{
  "dom_id": "step-q68177",
  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-7}+\\frac{1}{x+5}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-7}+\\frac{1}{x+5} & =\\frac{x+5+x-7}{(x-7)(x+5)}=\\frac{2x-2}{(x-7)(x+5)}=\\frac{2(x-1)}{(x-7)(x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-7)(x+5)=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=7\\,,\\,x=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=7$ and $x=-5.$",
